
==============================================================================
A. `sys.exit(charge(x))` FOR SIX INPUTS -- the EXIT STATUS a runner actually reads
==============================================================================
   bool-true   rc=3   | charge(True) = 3  type=int  verdict_of='UNASSIGNED (bool True aliases onto 1, not a verdict)'
   bool-false  rc=3   | charge(False) = 3  type=int  verdict_of='UNASSIGNED (bool False aliases onto 0, not a verdict)'
   float-1.0   rc=1   | charge(1.0) = 1.0  type=float  verdict_of='FAIL'
   float-0.0   rc=1   | charge(0.0) = 0.0  type=float  verdict_of='PASS'
   int-1       rc=1   | charge(1) = 1  type=int  verdict_of='FAIL'
   int-0       rc=0   | charge(0) = 0  type=int  verdict_of='PASS'

   what a runner reading `$?` would SAY:
   bool-true   -> REFUSED
   bool-false  -> REFUSED
   float-1.0   -> FAIL
   float-0.0   -> FAIL
   int-1       -> FAIL
   int-0       -> GREEN

==============================================================================
B. THE COMPARISON, STATED AS A TABLE. bool is closed; float is NOT.
==============================================================================
   input    exit rc  verdict to a reader    refused by the bool guard?
   True     3        REFUSED                YES
   False    3        REFUSED                YES
   1.0      1        FAIL                   no
   0.0      1        FAIL                   no
   1        1        FAIL                   no
   0        0        GREEN                  no

   `True`  -> REFUSED(3). The guard WORKS, and it is why `gatekit.charge(True)` reads 3.
   `1.0`   -> FAIL(1).   The guard does NOT apply: `isinstance(1.0, bool)` is False.
   `0.0`   -> FAIL(1).   NOT green, and I MEASURED WHY: CPython's `sys.exit` only accepts
                           an int or None -- a float is printed to stderr (`0.0`) and the
                           process exits 1. So the float's damage is FALSE-RED plus a
                           stray line on the stream the verdict was supposed to be on,
                           which is a worse nuisance than a wrong number alone.
                           **A DRAFT OF THIS SECTION CLAIMED `0.0` EXITS GREEN. IT DOES
                           NOT, AND MY OWN TABLE ABOVE ALREADY SAID SO.** Corrected
                           here rather than in the report, because the transcript is
                           the witness.

   AND THE FALSE-GREEN IS A DIFFERENT LINE, NOT THE SAME FUNCTION:
   `sys.exit(False)` with NO charge() guard -> rc 0  <-- GREEN, SILENTLY
   `bool` is accepted by `sys.exit` as an int and `False` IS 0. `charge()` is what
   stops it reaching a runner -- and every OTHER exit in the tree is unguarded.
   MEASURED over checks/ + gates/: 147 `sys.exit(...)` sites, of which 0 name `charge`. The bool guard is real and it is ONE FUNCTION'S WIDTH --
   and the tree already types an exit in exactly one place, which is not a boundary.
   CONSUMERS, MEASURED: 43 files import `gatekit`, 3 call
   `verdict_of`, and **0 call `charge`**. The fix landed inside the
   OWNER's own vocabulary. That is the correct place for it and it is still not a
   boundary: the 42 importers get `PASS/FAIL/...` as bare module constants and call
   `sys.exit(main())` with whatever `main()` happened to return.

==============================================================================
C. THE ONE-LINE CLOSE, AND WHY IT IS NOT LANDED HERE
==============================================================================
   `charge` would close the class with `type(code) is int` instead of
   `not isinstance(code, bool)`:
   charge_type_is_int(0     ) -> 0        charge(0     ) -> 0
   charge_type_is_int(1     ) -> 1        charge(1     ) -> 1
   charge_type_is_int(3     ) -> 3        charge(3     ) -> 3
   charge_type_is_int(True  ) -> 3        charge(True  ) -> 3
   charge_type_is_int(False ) -> 3        charge(False ) -> 3
   charge_type_is_int(1.0   ) -> 3        charge(1.0   ) -> 1.0
   charge_type_is_int(0.0   ) -> 3        charge(0.0   ) -> 0.0
   charge_type_is_int(2     ) -> 3        charge(2     ) -> 3
   charge_type_is_int('0'   ) -> 3        charge('0'   ) -> 3
   charge_type_is_int(None  ) -> 3        charge(None  ) -> 3

   It agrees with `charge` on every int -- so it is the SAME 0-consumer change -- and it
   differs on every bool AND every float. It is not landed here for one measured reason:
   IT IS A GATE BODY, AND THIS UNIT OWNS NO GATE BODIES. Reporting it is the honest
   move; landing it here would be the `AGENTS.md` class-1 defect in reverse -- a unit
   editing an instrument it did not census.

==============================================================================
D. WHAT `type(x) is int` COSTS, MEASURED -- because a 'better' fix that breaks a caller
==============================================================================
   is worse than the defect it fixes:
   isinstance(E.FAIL, int)   : True   charge(<E.FAIL: 1>) -> <E.FAIL: 1>
   type(E.FAIL) is int      : False
   So `type(x) is int` REFUSES an IntEnum verdict, which `isinstance` accepts.
   `exitcode`'s `not isinstance(code, bool)` is the SHORTER-RANGE choice and it is
   defensible; the point of this section is that its range is one member wide, and the
   tree now MEASURES that rather than assuming it.
